usage() {
    echo "usage: $0 [OPTIONS] COMMAND

The worldfile should contain the explicit list of packages that you want on your system!
Everything behind a '#' is considered as a comment (so whole lines, or end-of line comments).

We do allow a space limited list of packages in one line in the worldfile!
(they will be split and listed each in their own line in the KEEPFILE)

An entry's comment may END with a SELECTOR -- the machines the entry is for -- and then a SET-UP:
    brew \"syncthing\"   # file sync  %DESKTOP+@myhost  setup: syncthing-setup --install
A selector is %TIER (TIER_COMMAND says this machine's) or @host (hostname -s), then +TERM (add)
or -TERM (remove), read left to right; no selector = every machine. Every command reads only the
lines whose selector names this machine. The set-up runs with -U, once the package is installed.

_______________________________________________________________________________________________________

COMMANDS:
    -h|--help == show this text

    -l        == list worldfile - INcluded
    -L        == list worldfile - EXcluded files (commmented out)
    //// FILTER PACKAGES BY # OF LEADING WHITESPACES:
    -l0       == list worldfile - INcluded, but only packages with NO whitespaces in front of them
    -l<x>     == list worldfile - INcluded, but only packages with <x> whitespaces in front of them
    -l<x>-<y> == list worldfile - INcluded, but only packages with x-y whitespaces in front of them

    -i  == list *INvalid* packages from worldfile (checks in-validity of output of -l)
    -I  == list only VALID packages from worldfile (checks validity of output of -l)

    -m  == list    \"missing packages\" in worldfile which are NOT installed
    -M  == INSTALL \"missing packages\" in worldfile which are NOT installed [w. question to confirm!!!]

    -c  == list    \"commented packages\" from worldfile which are installed
    -C  == REMOVE  \"commented packages\" from worldfile which are installed [w. question to confirm!!!]

    -o  == list    \"orphaned packages\" NOT claimed by the worldfile (or its dependencies)
    -O  == REMOVE  \"orphaned packages\" NOT claimed by the worldfile (or its dependencies) [w. question to confirm!!!]

    -s  == list    \"sections\" from worldfile (lines starting with \"###\") -- in the original order -- into less!

    -u  == list    \"set-ups\" of this machine's entries: ENTRY<tab>COMMAND, in order, each COMMAND once
    -U  == RUN     the set-ups whose package is installed (the others are named, skipped); one that
                   fails does not stop the rest. A set-up runs on EVERY -U: it must change nothing
                   when it is in place already

    --create-config [FILE] == print the default config, or write it to FILE (never over an existing one)
    --run-tests [FILTER]   == run the self-tests whose name contains FILTER (all without one)

CONFIG: \$WORLDFILE_CONFIG, else --config <FILE>, else the first that exists of
    $(config_search_paths | xargs | sed 's/ /, /g').
    With none, the defaults that --create-config prints apply -- DISTRO empty = detected from the OS.

COMMAND OPTION FOR \"LIST\" COMMANDS:
    If you provide the list command letter TWICE you'll get a NICE output: packagename + summary-description-of-package
    For example:
    -mm == list   \"missing packages\" nicely (packagename + summary)

COMMAND OPTION FOR \"REMOVE\" COMMANDS:
    --purge == if you provide this additional parameter, the remove will purge the config files

FOR DEBUGGING PURPOSES:
    -D|--debug == turn on debug output
    -r  == force rebuilding keepfile <<< NOW DONE IMPLICITLY, WHEN NEEDED!
    -R  == force rebuilding keepfile to stdout

    -d  == list differences (with vimdiff) between worldfile and explicitely installed packages

    -do == list    \"distro-orphaned packages\" from what the DISTRIBUTION thinks is orphaned (*independant* of this script)
    -dO == REMOVE  \"distro-orphaned packages\" from what the DISTRIBUTION thinks is orphaned (*independant* of this script)

##############################################################################

SOME TERMS:
    \"sections\"             == lines in the worldfile that start with at least 3 \"#\"
    \"missing packages\"     == packages in worldfile which are NOT yet installed
    \"commented packages\"   == packages commented in worldfile which are STILL installed
    \"orphaned packages\"    == packages NOT claimed by worldfile + its dependencies
                             == additional packages installed ontop of what is demanded by the worldfile

    \"distro-orphaned packages\"  == like \"orphaned packages\" but relative to what the DISTRO thinks!
                                     (so completely *independent* of this scripts functionality!)

DISTRIBUTION SPECIFIC REMARKS:
  'arch') Here the package names, /may/ contain the repository name, e.g. aur/... or extra/gvim

"|less
    exit 0    
}

